{{define "content"}}
<vox-hero eyebrow="Access control and audit" heading="Who can do what, and who did what" data-i18n-attr="heading,eyebrow">
  <p data-i18n>
    Give people exactly the access they need, give your automation its own
    credentials, and keep a record of every change.
  </p>
</vox-hero>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Roles are permission sets</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      A role is a named list of permissions &mdash; read nodes, deploy
      code, run orchestration, manage vulnerability providers. Give a
      person one role or several. They see the parts of the console their
      roles cover, and nothing else: no buttons that fail when clicked.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{screenshot "roles"}}

<vox-grid min="240px" gap="lg">
  <vox-card heading="People" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="people"></vox-icon>
    Accounts managed in the console, or signed in through your existing
    OIDC provider and mapped onto the same roles.
  </vox-card>
  <vox-card heading="Machines" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="key"></vox-icon>
    Service tokens scoped to just the permissions an integration needs.
    Shown once, revocable at any time.
  </vox-card>
  <vox-card heading="Everything else" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="activity-log"></vox-icon>
    Every consequential action recorded with its actor, filterable by
    category, action and who did it.
  </vox-card>
</vox-grid>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>An audit trail that names names</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      Every entry records who did it and when, so "who changed this group"
      is a question with an answer. Work done by a scheduled task or by
      the console itself is shown as exactly that, rather than being
      pinned on a placeholder account.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{screenshot "activity"}}

<vox-callout variant="tip" heading="Built to survive a lost laptop" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <p data-i18n>
    Sessions are short-lived and refresh in the background, so a token
    that leaks stops working on its own. When you need someone out
    immediately, revoking their session takes effect across every console
    instance at once rather than waiting for it to expire.
  </p>
</vox-callout>
{{end}}
